WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:20.000
Human imagination. Adaptive intelligence. Quesar by MLAI introduces assistant workflows, memory systems and developer tools with inspectable sources.

2
00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:40.000
A place to begin. Read the architecture. The website brings products, platform, developers, research, services and contact into one navigation.

3
00:00:40.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Three roles. One conversation. Abbey is a human-facing companion contract. Aviva is a direct expert mode. Abi coordinates intent, risk, context and response style.

4
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Abbey. Care in the interface. Thoughtful teaching, creative work and technical collaboration are the intended role. Actual assistant behavior depends on its configured backend.

5
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Aviva. Clarity with boundaries. Concrete next actions and explicit uncertainty define the local persona profile. A role alone does not establish model quality.

6
00:01:40.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Abi. Coordination, inspected. Local routing and scheduler helpers select a persona or controlled blend. Broader governance remains partial.

7
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:20.000
Memory. Keep its provenance. WDBX local Rust storage and retrieval components preserve durable records, vectors and causal history.

8
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Integrity. Truth needs evidence. Storage integrity does not make a statement true. Production multi-host WDBX deployment is not established by this website.

9
00:02:40.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Developers. Start with source. Explore the ABI Rust framework, local CLI and MCP contracts. Follow setup and verification guidance before connecting tools or memory.

10
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:20.000
APIs. State availability. Hosted Quesar APIs and platform SDKs are not published. The website points to local frameworks and contracts.

11
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:40.000
Two names. Two distinct scopes. Quesar is the MLAI website and broader direction. Quasar is the separately configured local website builder service.

12
00:03:40.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Quasar. Browser to your service. The Quasar screens call the configured service directly from the browser. This website does not spawn that service.

13
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:20.000
Connect. Choose an origin. Settings lets the browser reach a local service and check that it answers. The deployment may supply a default origin.

14
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:40.000
Pairing. A deliberate connection. The settings screen includes a pairing token. Saving a connection and revoking an online pairing are explicit operations.

15
00:04:40.000 --> 00:05:00.000
A new site. Begin with a prompt. The local service copies a Next.js template into its sites directory and runs a Claude generation job using that machine’s Anthropic credentials.

16
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:20.000
A local library. Read live sites. The sites screen reads generated sites from the configured local service, directly through the browser.

17
00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:40.000
Generation. Follow the feed. The site detail screen polls service feed and preview state every second. A generation is a job to inspect, not an invisible promise.

18
00:05:40.000 --> 00:06:00.000
Refinement. Send a change request. The site detail screen offers an edit prompt. The route follows generation, sends edits and previews the site on your machine.

19
00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:20.000
Preview. A separate opening. Preview launch obtains a ticket and checks the returned target against the configured service before opening it.

20
00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:40.000
Recovery. Uncertainty is visible. The connection API exposes uncertain mutation state and recovery so screens can offer Retry rather than silently assuming success.

21
00:06:40.000 --> 00:07:00.000
Research. Ideas beside evidence. Source-backed work covers memory, retrieval, orchestration and provenance, with implementation status beside the research.

22
00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:20.000
NYON. Deterministic worlds. NYON is a related Rust strategy game with a separate offline Galaxy Workshop sandbox. Desktop and browser qualification remain in progress.

23
00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:40.000
The open workshop. Build with the source. Public repositories, documentation and plugins invite exploration through each project’s own contribution workflow.

24
00:07:40.000 --> 00:08:00.000
The future studio. Visual orchestration. MLAI Studio is a proposed workspace for visual agent design, prompt experiments and inspecting context, memory and execution.

25
00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:20.000
Many modalities. Explicit boundaries. Text, images, audio, code and spatial environments are a direction for future collaboration with explicit data boundaries.

26
00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:40.000
MLAI Network. A proposed community. The future network would let builders share agents, plugins, research, projects and extensions. It remains proposed.

27
00:08:40.000 --> 00:09:00.000
Static and server. Different capabilities. The static website renders notices for server features. A configured server deployment is needed for server-backed capabilities.

28
00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:20.000
Model configuration. Honest availability. The site uses one model interface. When no provider is configured, its model state is not configured rather than an invented response.

29
00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:40.000
Services. Define the evidence. Explore the services and contact pages to discuss a project. Scope, verification and operational boundaries should be explicit.

30
00:09:40.000 --> 00:10:00.000
Build thoughtfully. quesar.cloud Explore the architecture. Follow the source. Define the evidence your project needs. Quesar by MLAI.
